on  and making sure that i was well hydrated  as well to make sure that i didn't  develop headaches so now let's talk  about some of the difficulties of  fasting and i specifically want to  address hunger because i think that's  like the number one thing that people  worry about when they want to do fasting  or start off with fasting is i'm going  to be hungry all the time or i'm going  to get hungrier and hungrier and  hungrier and actually contrary to what  many people would believe that's not the  case so hunger actually comes in waves  because of hormones and specifically a  hormone called ghrelin so your body is  actually very smart and it learns your  patterns when you eat during specific  times let's say you eat your breakfast  lunch and your dinner your body over  time has actually learned these habits  and your hormones spike they go up and  they go down and because i've actually  done the shorter fast for a long time  before doing this challenge i've i had  come to already learn that and  experience that for myself that if i  just focus on a project or do a workout  or do something while i'm hungry it will  go away it doesn't usually last very  long really it's not that you're going  to be hungry all the time it's not that  you're going to get hungrier and  hungrier this is actually something that  you can just kind of learn to maneuver  and the more you can recognize it and  practice it the easier it gets so i i  would really recommend doing shorter  fasts before doing something like this  because i think more than anything it  was more of a mental challenge because  there's definitely a degree of fomo that  comes with it especially these longer  challenges of oh my gosh they're eating  this wonderful meal and i can't share it  and there's that feeling of i'm missing  out i'm missing out on that food that  they're eating that i can't eat or the  fear of when am i going to be able to  eat again and for anybody that has ever  had the experience of actually going  hungry where having food is not an  option there's definitely an aspect of  fear and so being able to practice that  in a controlled environment where you're  the boss where you could stop if you  wanted to i think really can help to  strengthen your mind and so more than  anything this challenge has been more of  a mental spiritual challenge you don't  necessarily have to do  fasting as a spiritual practice but i  think it is a huge component and so it's  just been interesting all the lessons  that i have learned the things that i've  learned about myself and the eating  habits that i had before that i had  never really noticed overall i think  this was a great experience and i think  one thing that can actually be really  helpful as well  a tip that i'd have for you is to let  the people around you know about what  you're doing and why you're doing it  because if they know that your purpose  is to help your body  maybe they won't be inclined to surprise  you with food or cook something because  i know for me  if my husband cooks something i think  it's really special i find it as an act  of love and so i feel rude to not eat  and so i actually let him know hey i'm  going to fast today so that he would  have kind of that warning like don't be  offering me food because i'm not going  to eat it and that really helped so that  would be a tip that i'd have for you is  to have an accountability partner or  just let the people around you know i  think the number one tip that i'd have  is if you are thinking of having an  extended pass is to schedule it during a  time that it will be  easy i guess if you can consider it easy  to avoid food for me what i did was i  did it kind of like evening time the day  before and then the next day i would be  working and so it would be hard to  actually have food because for me i'm a  home nurse anyway so if i ate it would  mean i would either pack my lunch or i'd  go to a restaurant physically and get  food and i don't ever do that anyway so  the only thing that i had to do was just  not pack a lunch and that would mean  that i'd have no temptation and because  i'm a home nurse it actually makes it  easier because i don't have other people  offering me food either there's no  co-workers there's no break time or  anything like that so for you if you are  thinking about this i would say just  think about your time wisely because it  really makes a big difference overall i  think this experience has been great um  i don't think that i would be doing it  on a weekly basis but i do definitely  see myself doing it again later on like  every couple of weeks or something like  that because i do find it as a reset  period for my body i feel better and  just overall the psychological aspects  that come with eating because i think  our relationship to food should not be  that a fear should not be that of guilt  or anxiety and it can really help to to  free you from that when you do  intermittent fasting either on a regular  basis or extended pass kind of  periodically so at least for me it was  very helpful truthfully this whole  challenge has been a surprise i honestly  expected that i'd be telling you more  physical results like that my eczema is  healed and no not really i still have it  so it shows you that i still have  inflammation however i do still see  fasting as a part of my life probably  not 24 hours every single week but i do  still see it in my future so hopefully  sharing my experience has been helpful  for you and that i haven't rambled too  much i'm sorry if i've rambled there's  just so much to be said about this topic  but if it's been helpful in any way  please give it a thumbs up and help  support my channel and let me know what  challenges you'd like to see me try i  thank you for watching and i can't wait  to hear from you bye  you
 

